00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:12.850
missed it.

00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:19.870
I've missed a lot of what I was doing there,
but also I want to modify it in a few different

00:11:19.870 --> 00:11:20.870
ways.

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:26.130
Tim Ferriss: Now this is separate from — unless
I'm already, I've had a third of a glass of

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:28.430
wine, so maybe I'm just hallucinating already.

00:11:28.430 --> 00:11:29.430
But Foundation?

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:32.420
Kevin Rose: The Foundation and Kevin Rose
Show kind of merged into one.

00:11:32.420 --> 00:11:36.300
So it's still one feed because Foundation
was all about interviewing entrepreneurs,

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:40.860
talking about their entrepreneurial journey,
trials, tribulations, things of that nature.

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:45.211
Tim Ferriss: Speaking about other planets,
you did have Elon on at one point.

00:11:45.211 --> 00:11:46.269
This is back in the day.

00:11:46.269 --> 00:11:50.620
Kevin Rose: Yeah, so that's actually what
I want to get back to where, when I think

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:58.191
about some of the value that I was able to
add to my listeners, it was around, and something

00:11:58.191 --> 00:12:01.760
that I think I've personally been good at
is identifying trends early on and talking

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:03.410
about them before anyone else.

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:05.900
Tim Ferriss: Just to underscore that.

00:12:05.900 --> 00:12:10.662
You are undeniably one of the best in the
world I've ever seen at that.

00:12:10.662 --> 00:12:11.662
Kevin Rose: I appreciate that, man.

00:12:11.662 --> 00:12:12.940
That means a lot coming from you, man.

00:12:12.940 --> 00:12:13.940
Tim Ferriss: It's true.

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:14.940
It's true.

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:17.149
I've seen it over and over and over and over
and over again.

00:12:17.149 --> 00:12:18.250
Yeah, it's incredible.

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:19.720
Kevin Rose: It's funny.

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:23.570
Someone has a clip of me mentioning Ethereum
for the first time on your show.

00:12:23.570 --> 00:12:24.570
Have you seen that?

00:12:24.570 --> 00:12:25.649
We did a Random Show.

00:12:25.649 --> 00:12:27.490
Tim Ferriss: It was like 1985.

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:31.470
I mean, yeah, it was way back in my apartment
in San Francisco.

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:34.010
Kevin Rose: I'm like, "Let me tell you about
this thing called Ethereum."

00:12:34.010 --> 00:12:35.010
Totally.

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:40.399
So, getting back to that, just really wanting
to highlight and expose people to that early

00:12:40.399 --> 00:12:43.459
beta — beta, I think of early software.

00:12:43.459 --> 00:12:49.080
And so the tentative working title's called
Beta, but it's going to be showing off things

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:53.220
as early as possible that have potentially
a lot of upside for the users.

00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:54.220
So, advancements in AI.

00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:58.170
When I had Elon on the show back in the day,
Tesla stock was $1.90.

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:01.850
I looked up the price of when he actually
came on the show.

00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:03.000
Tim Ferriss: Holy shit.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:04.870
Kevin Rose: Which is crazy.

00:13:04.870 --> 00:13:06.190
So it's going to be a variety show.

00:13:06.190 --> 00:13:08.190
It's going to cover tech, investing, health.

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:13.700
And then I think also important to throw in
there is this kind of mental work-life balance

00:13:13.700 --> 00:13:17.940
that is so important to me these days where
it's, yes, investing is great, yes, making

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:21.450
money is great, but it's not going to bring
you happiness unless you can figure out the

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:22.450
shit in your head.

00:13:22.450 --> 00:13:25.840
So, just making sure that's a component to
it as well.

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:30.100
Anyway, don't have anything to announce, but
I would tell people, by the time this show

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:34.470
comes out, if you just go to kevinrose.com,
you'll see a link to the podcast there.

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:35.470
Tim Ferriss: Have fun.

00:13:35.470 --> 00:13:36.610
Kevin Rose: And I'm going to fire it back
up.

00:13:36.610 --> 00:13:43.180
Tim Ferriss: Do you have any teasers for likely
or possible guests?

00:13:43.180 --> 00:13:45.889
Could be names, could be just profiles.

00:13:45.889 --> 00:13:47.250
Kevin Rose: It's a great question.

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:52.230
I had sent Elon an email a while ago and he
said he had a lot of fun on my first show

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:56.000
and was game to do it again.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:58.790
That was a while ago, but I got to go and
hit him back up.

00:13:58.790 --> 00:14:00.020
Would definitely want to have Elon back on.

00:14:00.020 --> 00:14:01.980
That's going to be a good one.

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:05.560
But I would say there's three or four, but
nobody is confirmed.

00:14:05.560 --> 00:14:08.339
I really want to kick it off with a good banger
to begin.

00:14:08.339 --> 00:14:10.540
So, TBD.

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:13.839
Tim Ferriss: TBD.

00:14:13.839 --> 00:14:23.350
Well, let me tell you about something exciting
on my side, which is really just a celebration

00:14:23.350 --> 00:14:25.620
at this point of enthusiasm for me.

00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:30.930
It's not a launch or anything like that at
this point, but you don't know about this.

00:14:30.930 --> 00:14:36.940
Nobody knows about this really, which is I
recently, last week, did a, I guess it was

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:47.430
a four-day creative sprint in the middle of
nowhere at this rustic retreat with two writers

00:14:47.430 --> 00:14:52.010
and three concept artists who are some of
the best in the world, who have done some

00:14:52.010 --> 00:14:59.740
of the best-known work for D&amp;D, for Magic:
The Gathering, and we worked on CØCKPUNCH

00:14:59.740 --> 00:15:00.800
stuff, believe it or not.

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:01.800
Kevin Rose: Amazing.

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:06.820
Tim Ferriss: We actually spent that entire
time coming up with scenarios, characters,

00:15:06.820 --> 00:15:10.480
adventures, concept sketches, artwork.

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:11.610
I don't want to spoil the surprise.

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:17.959
We made some significant tweaks to the nature
of that realm that I think are very, very,

00:15:17.959 --> 00:15:24.910
very compelling and got tons and tons of artwork,
and it's spectacular.

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:28.500
Some of the work is just outrageously, outrageously
good.

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:34.339
What these guys can do in a handful of hours,
it boggles my mind.

00:15:34.339 --> 00:15:37.250
It is just beyond my comprehension.

00:15:37.250 --> 00:15:42.700
It's really as if, from the writing perspective,
if someone sat down and in three hours, they

00:15:42.700 --> 00:15:46.889
just banged out 3,000 perfect words, I'd be
like, "What?

00:15:46.889 --> 00:15:48.430
How's that humanly possible?"

00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:49.860
I saw that with these artists.

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:55.500
They were so well-oiled, so professional,
and also had very different styles, which

00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:56.500
was super-fun.

00:15:56.500 --> 00:16:00.269
So, I could watch them also feeding off one
another even though they were working on different

00:16:00.269 --> 00:16:04.720
aspects, different greater houses and so on.

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:10.000
For me, part of the celebration is realizing
that even though it was my first go-around,

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:16.829
my first rodeo, and I didn't quite know what
I was doing of course, it was an experiment

00:16:16.829 --> 00:16:18.870
in collaboration because I've been such a
solo act.

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:24.920
I've been such a soloist when it comes to
large creative projects.

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:31.430
Having an opportunity to work with, say, not
just one 3D artist, which I did, Walter, for,

00:16:31.430 --> 00:16:37.120
say, the initial three-dimensional, 3D Blender
character designs.

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:43.600
But to work with an entire group and to see
if I can actually interact or co-manage that

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:46.580
process in some way was very reassuring.

00:16:46.580 --> 00:16:47.580
I think I can do it.

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:56.500
I think that even if I'm not putting pen to
paper or stylus to a tablet in some fashion,

00:16:56.500 --> 00:17:04.819
I think the past that I have and the experience
that I have with line art and more recently

00:17:04.819 --> 00:17:11.550
with painting and so on, really helps me to
give better feedback to artists and to notice

00:17:11.550 --> 00:17:12.730
details.

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:14.459
And I'm very excited about it.

00:17:14.459 --> 00:17:16.549
Kevin Rose: I mean, there's a few things I
want to comment on here.

00:17:16.549 --> 00:17:23.760
One, it's awesome to see, and it's so apparent
to me now that you launched this as an NFT

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:27.000
project and NFTs are having a bad time right
now.

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:29.590
Tim Ferriss: It's definitely one way to put
it.

00:17:29.590 --> 00:17:31.840
Kevin Rose: It's a bloody market out there.

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:34.809
All the proceeds went to a nonprofit.

00:17:34.809 --> 00:17:36.700
Royalties are dead.

00:17:36.700 --> 00:17:40.750
So the idea that you're making any further
money on this via NFTs is not the case.

00:17:40.750 --> 00:17:41.750
Tim Ferriss: Basically zero, yep.

00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:45.309
Kevin Rose: And so you're doing this for the
love of the game, which I love to see, which

00:17:45.309 --> 00:17:46.309
is awesome.

00:17:46.309 --> 00:17:52.140
And the second more important thing, having
known you for a long, long time is I've never

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:58.340
seen you — I've seen you struggle with giving
up a little bit of creative control, like

00:17:58.340 --> 00:18:00.880
delegation.

00:18:00.880 --> 00:18:03.500
So I'll give you some old Tim Ferriss stories.

00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:07.850
Every time Tim would write a new book, I'd
be like, "Dude, Tim, let me get a little early

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:08.850
copy.

00:18:08.850 --> 00:18:09.850
Let me get a little chapter.

00:18:09.850 --> 00:18:10.850
Let me see what you're working on.

00:18:10.850 --> 00:18:11.850
What hacks are you doing?"

00:18:11.850 --> 00:18:12.850
Blah, blah, blah.

00:18:12.850 --> 00:18:14.490
You wouldn't share that shit with anybody.

00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:17.080
You're so secretive.

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:19.010
You'd be like, "How do I encrypt my hard drive?"

00:18:19.010 --> 00:18:24.830
You would try and lock down all your shit,
but to see you move into this world of more

00:18:24.830 --> 00:18:26.350
of creative director is awesome.

00:18:26.350 --> 00:18:28.460
Has that been a challenge for you?

00:18:28.460 --> 00:18:30.330
Am I articulating that correctly?

00:18:30.330 --> 00:18:31.330
Tim Ferriss: You are.

00:18:31.330 --> 00:18:32.940
Yeah, you're definitely articulating correctly.

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:38.419
I mean, I think as we were talking about before
we started recording, we both have some maybe

00:18:38.419 --> 00:18:42.460
daddy trust issues or whatever that we've
needed to work through on a lot of fronts

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:46.570
and it's definitely the case that that's true.

00:18:46.570 --> 00:18:57.260
I will say that there were a few decisions
that were super-key in facilitating, lubricating,

00:18:57.260 --> 00:19:03.370
if you will, the possibility of me acting
in a more creative director role.

00:19:03.370 --> 00:19:16.330
So, the first was, and it's easy to miss the
importance of this with the absurdity of the

00:19:16.330 --> 00:19:17.700
name of the project.

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:20.850
CØCKPUNCH, The Legend of CØCKPUNCH.

00:19:20.850 --> 00:19:23.720
People should check out the trailer at the
very least on the podcast.

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:25.280
Kevin Rose: Oh, my God.

00:19:25.280 --> 00:19:26.280
Episode one.

00:19:26.280 --> 00:19:27.460
Tim Ferriss: It's so good.

00:19:27.460 --> 00:19:34.549
Some amazing voice actors involved and the
key there by using that name.

00:19:34.549 --> 00:19:38.980
And this was very deliberate and by pursuing
it the way I pursued it and also giving the

00:19:38.980 --> 00:19:46.330
funds to the Saisei Foundation, my foundation,
S-A-I-S-E-I Foundation.org for people who

00:19:46.330 --> 00:19:50.059
want to see the projects I'm involved with.

00:19:50.059 --> 00:19:57.190
What that did is it basically made fixating
on money kind of silly in the sense that it

00:19:57.190 --> 00:20:03.190
doesn't go to my pocket, it's going to the
Foundation, which is important, but it's not

00:20:03.190 --> 00:20:07.929
going to directly improve the quality of my
life in any way.

00:20:07.929 --> 00:20:17.870
Secondly, the fact that it was given such
an absurd/ hilarious title allowed me not

00:20:17.870 --> 00:20:21.570
to take it too seriously or be too precious
or protective of it.

00:20:21.570 --> 00:20:24.010
And that is what opened the door to this collaboration.

00:20:24.010 --> 00:20:31.630
My hope is that by testing this with something
like CØCKPUNCH, that I will then be able

00:20:31.630 --> 00:20:35.780
to translate it to things that I might be
inclined to be more protective with.

00:20:35.780 --> 00:20:42.549
There have been so many benefits to this,
and I'll name a few, or I'll at least lead

00:20:42.549 --> 00:20:47.600
with one, which is the energy that this has
generated has been unbelievable.

00:20:47.600 --> 00:20:52.340
It's been like a power plant for everything
else that I'm doing.

00:20:52.340 --> 00:21:00.299
So, it's charging my batteries so effectively
that I've been able to engage much more potently

00:21:00.299 --> 00:21:01.299
with everything else.

00:21:01.299 --> 00:21:02.850
Kevin Rose: This is creative energy?

00:21:02.850 --> 00:21:04.480
This is from the creativity of it all?

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:05.480
Tim Ferriss: Creative energy, creative energy.

00:21:05.480 --> 00:21:14.679
For instance, I mean the sort of admin paper-cutty
stuff that is so easy to succumb to, as a

00:21:14.679 --> 00:21:16.790
sort of death by a thousand paper cuts.

00:21:16.790 --> 00:21:17.950
Look, you need to do your taxes.

00:21:17.950 --> 00:21:24.190
There are things you need to do that you don't
really want to do, that deplete, at least

00:21:24.190 --> 00:21:26.420
in my case, kind of deplete my batteries.

00:21:26.420 --> 00:21:31.440
And if you don't have something on the other
side of the ledger to recharge those batteries,

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:35.100
you can end up being really fatigued.

00:21:35.100 --> 00:21:40.700
You just don't have extra calories to allocate
to a lot of things.

00:21:40.700 --> 00:21:45.740
This silly project, which has achieved a lot
of serious things, I mean, millions of dollars

00:21:45.740 --> 00:21:48.760
have gone to the Foundation, or at least two
million bucks, and there's more that's going

00:21:48.760 --> 00:21:58.919
to go because soon I'll be donating all the
secondary royalty revenue also to the Foundation.

00:21:58.919 --> 00:22:03.400
I mean, it's done a lot of good in terms of
supporting early-stage science at all sorts

00:22:03.400 --> 00:22:08.410
of top universities and journalism fellowships
at UC Berkeley and so on.

00:22:08.410 --> 00:22:09.940
But check this out.

00:22:09.940 --> 00:22:11.169
This'll be another connection.

00:22:11.169 --> 00:22:18.039
And that is I literally, and we'll see if
I can keep this in or not.

00:22:18.039 --> 00:22:20.159
I need to talk to the other person.

00:22:20.159 --> 00:22:21.919
Kevin Rose: That's how you know it's good.

00:22:21.919 --> 00:22:22.919
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:22:22.919 --> 00:22:29.490
I just signed my first-ever writing collaboration
agreement to work on my first book in six

00:22:29.490 --> 00:22:32.049
years with another person.

00:22:32.049 --> 00:22:33.840
I've never done that before in my life.

00:22:33.840 --> 00:22:35.170
And that's my baby, right?

00:22:35.170 --> 00:22:36.170
I mean —
Kevin Rose: Is this fiction?

00:22:36.170 --> 00:22:37.430
Is this going to be —
Tim Ferriss: This is nonfiction.

00:22:37.430 --> 00:22:38.430
Kevin Rose: Nonfiction.

00:22:38.430 --> 00:22:46.029
Tim Ferriss: It'll be sort of old-school Tim
Ferriss in a sense, because it'll be what

00:22:46.029 --> 00:22:50.020
I'm probably best at and most known for, but
it will be done with someone.

00:22:50.020 --> 00:22:51.020
Kevin Rose: Oh.

00:22:51.020 --> 00:22:52.020
You adding an hour to the week?

00:22:52.020 --> 00:22:53.679
Tim Ferriss: Inflation, baby.

00:22:53.679 --> 00:22:54.679
Inflation hits us all.

00:22:54.679 --> 00:22:57.830
It's not going to be The 5-Hour Workweek.

00:22:57.830 --> 00:23:06.280
Kevin Rose: What's funny is you can release
that and people will be like, "Oh, shit, he

00:23:06.280 --> 00:23:09.390
figured out something new," and it'd be like
a bestseller.

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:13.190
Tim Ferriss: The first three or four hours
are all about work, and the fifth hour is

00:23:13.190 --> 00:23:14.190
all about pleasure.

00:23:14.190 --> 00:23:15.190
Here we go, folks.

00:23:15.190 --> 00:23:16.190
Kevin Rose: Exactly.

00:23:16.190 --> 00:23:17.570
Tim Ferriss: Epicurean 2.0.

00:23:17.570 --> 00:23:26.480
So, I am beyond excited about this because
this is now segueing from cutting my teeth

00:23:26.480 --> 00:23:30.550
on some collaboration with this thing called
CØCKPUNCH.

00:23:30.550 --> 00:23:41.220
But now I've segued to my actual bailiwick,
right down the middle with hardcore nonfiction,

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:44.450
super-tactical nonfiction, and I'm going to
work with someone else.

00:23:44.450 --> 00:23:50.870
And I don't think there's any chance I would
have been able to do that had I not deliberately

00:23:50.870 --> 00:23:56.390
created something that I didn't feel too protective
or precious about.

00:23:56.390 --> 00:23:58.460
So, I'm fucking stoked, man.

00:23:58.460 --> 00:23:59.460
I'm really excited.

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:01.529
Kevin Rose: Let me ask you a question.

00:24:01.529 --> 00:24:08.720
How do you decide to, and as a general tip
for the audience out there, how and when do

00:24:08.720 --> 00:24:16.720
you decide to outsource things to other people
that you would consider to be representative

00:24:16.720 --> 00:24:22.539
of yourself or your brand or in some way could
be potentially damaging?

00:24:22.539 --> 00:24:28.990
I think about, this has been a hard one for
me because anytime an app ships or anytime

00:24:28.990 --> 00:24:34.470
anything happens that is associated with a
company that I have started, if there's something

00:24:34.470 --> 00:24:39.020
breaks or it's not done the correct way, it's
always like, "Oh, Kevin, why didn't you catch

00:24:39.020 --> 00:24:40.020
this?"

00:24:40.020 --> 00:24:41.020
Blah blah?

00:24:41.020 --> 00:24:42.020
And I'm like, "I did, there's 15 other people…"

00:24:42.020 --> 00:24:45.310
Tim Ferriss: That's what other people say
to you, or that's what you say to you?

00:24:45.310 --> 00:24:46.600
Kevin Rose: Both.

00:24:46.600 --> 00:24:48.160
They somehow think I'm doing everything.

00:24:48.160 --> 00:24:50.770
And so they'll be like, "It's impossible that
you didn't catch this."

00:24:50.770 --> 00:24:54.400
And I'm like, "I didn't see it before it went
out."

00:24:54.400 --> 00:25:02.330
And so I'm wondering, because your brand is
so important to protect, who has final edit

00:25:02.330 --> 00:25:03.470
on your podcast?

00:25:03.470 --> 00:25:05.460
How did you trust them with that?

00:25:05.460 --> 00:25:06.460
Because —
Tim Ferriss: Totally.

00:25:06.460 --> 00:25:07.460
This is a great question.

00:25:07.460 --> 00:25:10.340
Kevin Rose: Otherwise, you'd have to listen
to every single podcast you ever did in great

00:25:10.340 --> 00:25:11.340
detail.

00:25:11.340 --> 00:25:12.340
And it's like, yeah.

00:25:12.340 --> 00:25:13.340
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, we've never talked about
this.

00:25:13.340 --> 00:25:14.340
This is fun.

00:25:14.340 --> 00:25:18.450
All right, I've never talked about this publicly,
so I am slow to give up the reins.

00:25:18.450 --> 00:25:23.960
I'm very slow, and I'm slow to trust in general,
as you know.

00:25:23.960 --> 00:25:25.110
There's some upside to that.

00:25:25.110 --> 00:25:28.260
There are also a bunch of downsides.

00:25:28.260 --> 00:25:30.840
But love it or hate it, that is where we are.

00:25:30.840 --> 00:25:34.860
I'm very hypervigilant in that way.

00:25:34.860 --> 00:25:42.029
In the case of the podcast, first step after
each podcast is recorded, before I even send

00:25:42.029 --> 00:25:46.282
my edit notes or anything like that, is the
podcast is transcribed and there are some

00:25:46.282 --> 00:25:51.940
great tools out there that can interact with
transcripts that we have also tested and used

00:25:51.940 --> 00:25:53.299
in the past, like Descript.

00:25:53.299 --> 00:25:54.630
Kevin Rose: We use Descript too.

00:25:54.630 --> 00:25:55.630
Tim Ferriss: Right.

00:25:55.630 --> 00:25:56.630
Which is really helpful.

00:25:56.630 --> 00:26:01.260
For people who don't know, it allows you to,
say, strip out all the ums and ahs and filler

00:26:01.260 --> 00:26:07.370
words automatically, and you make text edits
that then get translated into audio edits.

00:26:07.370 --> 00:26:08.460
It's very interesting.

00:26:08.460 --> 00:26:15.020
I also use professional sound editors and
so on, but step one for all of that is getting

00:26:15.020 --> 00:26:16.020
the audio transcribed.

00:26:16.020 --> 00:26:25.130
So we get the audio transcribed, and at this
point I have something like 700 episodes,

00:26:25.130 --> 00:26:30.879
close to 700 episodes, which for those who
are wondering is, it's pretty crazy.

00:26:30.879 --> 00:26:35.180
Next April, I think it's next April is going
to be 10 years of the podcast.

00:26:35.180 --> 00:26:36.180
Can you fucking believe that?

00:26:36.180 --> 00:26:37.180
It's crazy.

00:26:37.180 --> 00:26:38.180
Kevin Rose: That's insane.

00:26:38.180 --> 00:26:41.330
Tim Ferriss: 10 years, 1.4 episodes on average
per week, for 10 years straight.

00:26:41.330 --> 00:26:42.330
That's insane.

00:26:42.330 --> 00:26:43.330
Kevin Rose: Damn.

00:26:43.330 --> 00:26:44.330
Did you have hair when you started?

00:26:44.330 --> 00:26:45.330
I can't even remember.

00:26:45.330 --> 00:26:46.330
I don't think.

00:26:46.330 --> 00:26:47.330
Tim Ferriss: I probably had a little more
hair.

00:26:47.330 --> 00:26:50.730
My hair probably looked like my beard right
now, so it wasn't much.

00:26:50.730 --> 00:26:51.731
Kevin Rose: I definitely didn't have gray
hair.

00:26:51.731 --> 00:26:52.731
I didn't have gray hair back then.

00:26:52.731 --> 00:26:54.430
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I wasn't going to be on
any of those poster boards where they show

00:26:54.430 --> 00:26:56.260
you all the haircuts that are possible at
the barber.

00:26:56.260 --> 00:26:57.260
It's very old school.

00:26:57.260 --> 00:26:59.559
Go with the flat top, I want that one.

00:26:59.559 --> 00:27:03.289
I wouldn't have been on any of those, but
I probably had a little more hair.

00:27:03.289 --> 00:27:04.289
I was probably faking it.

00:27:04.289 --> 00:27:09.330
I think I was probably at the last, the 11th
hour of my white-knuckling.

00:27:09.330 --> 00:27:12.460
I probably had some cheesy faux hawk, because
that's all that was left and I was trying

00:27:12.460 --> 00:27:13.570
to cover up bald spots.

00:27:13.570 --> 00:27:15.039
Something like that.

00:27:15.039 --> 00:27:22.059
In any case, the point I was going to make
is that my current general manager of the

00:27:22.059 --> 00:27:29.330
podcast, who's really the COO of editorial,
has been working for me for seven or eight

00:27:29.330 --> 00:27:30.330
years.

00:27:30.330 --> 00:27:32.049
We've been working together a long time.

00:27:32.049 --> 00:27:38.081
Over that period of time, we've now done a
few hundred episodes together, minimum, and

00:27:38.081 --> 00:27:46.970
I started with making edits myself in a Google
Doc and then we would go back and forth.

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:52.049
Over time, I got to the point where I would
ask him to suggest edits and then I would

00:27:52.049 --> 00:27:56.100
go through and provide refinements and feedback.

00:27:56.100 --> 00:28:03.679
And I am at the point now where we probably
have 95 percent overlap, which is good enough.

00:28:03.679 --> 00:28:12.269
And I therefore feel very comfortable with
letting him make the vast majority of edit

00:28:12.269 --> 00:28:13.669
decisions related to the podcast.

00:28:13.669 --> 00:28:14.669
Kevin Rose: That's awesome.

00:28:14.669 --> 00:28:15.669
How long did that take you?

00:28:15.669 --> 00:28:16.669
Was that a year process?

00:28:16.669 --> 00:28:17.970
Tim Ferriss: At least a year.

00:28:17.970 --> 00:28:19.020
Yeah, at least a year.

00:28:19.020 --> 00:28:23.799
Because you're really training someone to
do two things.

00:28:23.799 --> 00:28:31.409
One is to understand how you think well enough,
my decision-making process, so that they can

00:28:31.409 --> 00:28:36.470
step into the shoes or into the mind of Tim
Ferriss to look at a transcript.

00:28:36.470 --> 00:28:43.550
The second is to be the best version of their
editorial selves for using judgment, and it's

00:28:43.550 --> 00:28:46.620
the combination that works really well.

00:28:46.620 --> 00:28:56.340
So I would say it took me, probably in his
case, his responsibilities expanded dramatically

00:28:56.340 --> 00:28:58.960
as we worked together over time.

00:28:58.960 --> 00:29:05.080
But I would say since he has effectively run
the operation side of the podcast, probably

00:29:05.080 --> 00:29:08.640
took a year and a half to two years is what
I would say.

00:29:08.640 --> 00:29:17.450
And I think it can be done much more quickly
for people who really focus on it and who

00:29:17.450 --> 00:29:20.179
don't have the trust issues that I have.

00:29:20.179 --> 00:29:26.159
I think if you have a high enough volume of
podcasts, which I have directly throttled.

00:29:26.159 --> 00:29:32.810
I used to do, say, at times, six, seven episodes
a month because you have all of these other

00:29:32.810 --> 00:29:38.610
interview-based podcasts that are doing four
or five a week, and I felt like that was a

00:29:38.610 --> 00:29:44.020
trend that I should — not a trend I should
follow, but that was certainly in my economic

00:29:44.020 --> 00:29:48.720
best interest to publish more episodes and
people were consuming them.

00:29:48.720 --> 00:29:54.510
But I started to drag my feet and realized
it was starting to feel like a J-O-B in the

00:29:54.510 --> 00:30:00.380
sense that I really was not looking forward
to my conversations after the fourth or fifth

00:30:00.380 --> 00:30:02.360
or sixth of a month.

00:30:02.360 --> 00:30:06.720
So I dialed back deliberately so that I would
still enjoy what I was doing.

00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:10.480
What that meant, though, is that you're getting
fewer iterations or you're getting fewer at-bat

00:30:10.480 --> 00:30:15.810
practices with the person you're trying to
train to be a world-class editor.

00:30:15.810 --> 00:30:21.370
And I think you could do it, if you had decent
volume, you could probably do it in three

00:30:21.370 --> 00:30:22.370
months.

00:30:22.370 --> 00:30:26.909
Especially, for instance, one thing that I
could have done, I just didn't think of it

00:30:26.909 --> 00:30:28.930
and I didn't have really the bandwidth.

00:30:28.930 --> 00:30:33.210
I'm just juggling a lot and it wasn't my absolute
top priority.

00:30:33.210 --> 00:30:34.680
You don't have to use new audio.

00:30:34.680 --> 00:30:44.460
I could have said, "Let's take the raw audio
from 20 episodes that were published before,"

00:30:44.460 --> 00:30:49.100
or if you have a friend who's a podcaster,
you could have said, "Give me 20 of your episodes,"

00:30:49.100 --> 00:30:51.850
and then have him run through it.

00:30:51.850 --> 00:30:56.409
And then I would run through it, and boom,
before you know it, if the person is reasonably

00:30:56.409 --> 00:30:59.440
adaptive and perceptive, they'll figure it
out.

00:30:59.440 --> 00:31:04.601
So I would say three to six months, but I'm
at a point now where it's at least 95 percent

00:31:04.601 --> 00:31:05.601
overlap.

00:31:05.601 --> 00:31:09.760
And if there's something that's very nuanced
that I feel strongly about, I will still make

00:31:09.760 --> 00:31:10.760
that edit myself.

00:31:10.760 --> 00:31:15.140
And some of it is really, really, really,
really nuanced.

00:31:15.140 --> 00:31:19.919
Let's just say a guest gives a long answer.

00:31:19.919 --> 00:31:24.200
In the middle of that answer, they say something
that's factually incorrect.

00:31:24.200 --> 00:31:29.789
And for my audience and also for the guest
themselves, I want to remove that.

00:31:29.789 --> 00:31:34.210
But if I remove it, perhaps that removes a
bunch of important context.

00:31:34.210 --> 00:31:39.549
So I may need to take something from elsewhere
in the conversation and slot it in or create

00:31:39.549 --> 00:31:46.920
a hybrid sort of Frankenstein for it to make
sense, or ask them to do a pickup, meaning

00:31:46.920 --> 00:31:50.880
record additional audio to slot in somewhere.

00:31:50.880 --> 00:31:56.980
That is at a level of complexity and also
Tim Ferriss' subjective whim that I will often

00:31:56.980 --> 00:31:59.620
step in to try to make some of those decisions.

00:31:59.620 --> 00:32:02.370
But my right hand makes a lot of the decisions
on his own.

00:32:02.370 --> 00:32:03.370
Kevin Rose: That's amazing.

00:32:03.370 --> 00:32:04.520
Tim Ferriss: And it's incredible.

00:32:04.520 --> 00:32:06.010
So that's been the process.

00:32:06.010 --> 00:32:13.580
And there is, I will say, to put out a really
good finished episode, oftentimes there is

00:32:13.580 --> 00:32:20.350
a fuck-ton of work that happens behind the
scenes, that if we do our jobs correctly,

00:32:20.350 --> 00:32:22.340
are completely invisible.

00:32:22.340 --> 00:32:29.940
Kevin Rose: For me, it's obviously a different
can of worms in running a startup in a business,

00:32:29.940 --> 00:32:36.470
but I think of it kind of through this lens
where one just a hundred percent, not even

00:32:36.470 --> 00:32:38.150
taking shortcuts when it comes to hiring.

00:32:38.150 --> 00:32:43.000
Just hiring absolute A+ players, or what you
believe to be A+ players, from day one.

00:32:43.000 --> 00:32:48.980
I really, when I was working at Google and
you walk in the door and you get to see who

00:32:48.980 --> 00:32:55.120
you're surrounded by and who your peers are,
it's very apparent that that is, there's a

00:32:55.120 --> 00:33:00.620
reason why their hiring process is so long,
and so kind of a little bit tedious, but just

00:33:00.620 --> 00:33:06.059
it's quite the complex — several hurdles
that you have to get over before you actually

00:33:06.059 --> 00:33:07.059
get in the door.

00:33:07.059 --> 00:33:12.180
And I understand why those hurdles are so
high now, because once you get in, they give

00:33:12.180 --> 00:33:17.120
you the keys to the castle and say, "Just
go run, make mistakes, learn," and they trust

00:33:17.120 --> 00:33:18.120
you a lot.

00:33:18.120 --> 00:33:24.490
And so for me, once we have — I know I have
high-quality talent to work with, it comes

00:33:24.490 --> 00:33:33.419
down to if this were to go completely sideways,
how big of screw up would it be publicly into

00:33:33.419 --> 00:33:34.500
our community?

00:33:34.500 --> 00:33:40.630
And if the answer is a six or above, I probably
want to give it a little bit more hands-on

00:33:40.630 --> 00:33:45.760
attention, and if the answer is an eight or
above, then I want to be heavily involved

00:33:45.760 --> 00:33:49.519
in that process.

00:33:49.519 --> 00:33:52.210
If it's a kind of five or below, who cares?

00:33:52.210 --> 00:33:56.269
Let them make the mistake, because you just
don't have time to look at everything.

00:33:56.269 --> 00:33:59.291
Tim Ferriss: Not only that, but you don't
want your employees, and look, I think you're

00:33:59.291 --> 00:34:02.220
better at this —
Kevin Rose: The micromanaging side.

00:34:02.220 --> 00:34:03.289
Tim Ferriss: Exactly.

00:34:03.289 --> 00:34:04.289
Exactly.

00:34:04.289 --> 00:34:07.279
It's like you don't want them to feel like
you're the teacher looking over their shoulder

00:34:07.279 --> 00:34:13.300
to point out every mistake they make as they're
working, which has been hard for me to learn,

00:34:13.300 --> 00:34:15.290
honestly, because I am such a perfectionist.

00:34:15.290 --> 00:34:16.590
I don't know if I told you this.

00:34:16.590 --> 00:34:21.250
Did I tell you that I was diagnosed with moderate/severe
OCD by a psychiatrist?

00:34:21.250 --> 00:34:23.130
Did I tell you this?

00:34:23.130 --> 00:34:24.260
Kevin Rose: No.

00:34:24.260 --> 00:34:25.260
Holy shit.

00:34:25.260 --> 00:34:27.129
That all makes sense now.

00:34:27.129 --> 00:34:32.860
Tim Ferriss: What was funny is that I went
through this long thing, and then the psychiatrist

00:34:32.860 --> 00:34:39.510
was like, "Okay, I feel like I have a very
confident read on our current state of play

00:34:39.510 --> 00:34:40.510
and the diagnosis."

00:34:40.510 --> 00:34:49.869
And so he tells me all this and he says, we're
on Zoom, and he's like, "I understand this

00:34:49.869 --> 00:34:51.020
could be a lot to take in.

00:34:51.020 --> 00:34:52.173
If you need time, we can take a break.

00:34:52.173 --> 00:34:56.190
If you want to hop off the call and pick up
tomorrow…" and I was like, "Are you fucking

00:34:56.190 --> 00:34:57.190
kidding me?

00:34:57.190 --> 00:34:58.190
I'm not surprised at all."

00:34:58.190 --> 00:34:59.190
Kevin Rose: Yeah.

00:34:59.190 --> 00:35:00.900
Tim Ferriss: I'm like, "Yeah, okay, fine.

00:35:00.900 --> 00:35:01.900
What's next?

00:35:01.900 --> 00:35:02.900
That's fine."

00:35:02.900 --> 00:35:03.900
Kevin Rose: Dude, it's so funny you say that.

00:35:03.900 --> 00:35:06.790
I've got an awesome new therapist that I'm
really enjoying.

00:35:06.790 --> 00:35:08.060
She's amazing.

00:35:08.060 --> 00:35:14.230
And she's like, "I want to refer you to someone
because what you're describing sounds like

00:35:14.230 --> 00:35:15.230
ADHD."

00:35:15.230 --> 00:35:17.400
And I'm like, "Yeah, no shit."

00:35:17.400 --> 00:35:18.430
Big surprise.

00:35:18.430 --> 00:35:19.430
Shocker.

00:35:19.430 --> 00:35:20.430
Shocker.

00:35:20.430 --> 00:35:21.520
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, shocker.

00:35:21.520 --> 00:35:24.490
Kevin Rose: It literally happened a week ago.

00:35:24.490 --> 00:35:25.490
It's amazing.

00:35:25.490 --> 00:35:26.839
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:35:26.839 --> 00:35:31.869
I reached out to a couple of my ex-girlfriends
who I'm friendly with, and I asked them, I

00:35:31.869 --> 00:35:33.240
was like, "Does this square with your experience?"

00:35:33.240 --> 00:35:34.619
And they're like, "Yeah, yeah.

00:35:34.619 --> 00:35:36.270
It squares with my experience."

00:35:36.270 --> 00:35:38.750
I was like, "Okay.

00:35:38.750 --> 00:35:39.750
Confirmed."

00:35:39.750 --> 00:35:45.960
And this was in, just to provide the context,
this was in the context of trying to determine

00:35:45.960 --> 00:35:53.810
neuroanatomical targets for something referred
to as accelerated TMS, which I'm hoping to

00:35:53.810 --> 00:36:00.579
talk quite a bit more about, but I don't feel
comfortable yet making any type of recommendation.

00:36:00.579 --> 00:36:08.790
But it is a new protocol with a very, I shouldn't
say very old, but like 1980s-ish, forward

00:36:08.790 --> 00:36:12.770
developing technology, transcranial magnetic
stimulation.

00:36:12.770 --> 00:36:22.910
So it's brain stimulation for the purposes
of minimizing or eliminating the symptoms

00:36:22.910 --> 00:36:31.859
of certain things like OCD, depression, generalized
— or maybe it's just general anxiety disorder.

00:36:31.859 --> 00:36:33.960
I think it's generalized anxiety disorder.

00:36:33.960 --> 00:36:34.960
GAD.

00:36:34.960 --> 00:36:39.359
And I become very interested in this because
there are data related to something that's

00:36:39.359 --> 00:36:45.349
been called the SAINT protocol, I think, that
had too many religious overtones so they abbreviated

00:36:45.349 --> 00:36:48.360
to, I think maybe SNT.

00:36:48.360 --> 00:36:52.890
It's a Stanford-based brain stimulation lab.

00:36:52.890 --> 00:36:56.740
There's some incredible scientists involved,
including Nolan Williams, who's incredibly

00:36:56.740 --> 00:37:02.800
impressive to me, who have developed this
protocol and the magnitude of effect that

00:37:02.800 --> 00:37:07.020
I have seen in some of the data, this does
not apply to all people, it does not apply

00:37:07.020 --> 00:37:12.460
to all conditions, but shows a magnitude of
effect that makes it equally as interesting

00:37:12.460 --> 00:37:14.750
to me as psychedelic-assisted therapies.

00:37:14.750 --> 00:37:22.069
It's the only thing I have come across in
the last five years that approaches the amplitude

00:37:22.069 --> 00:37:27.330
of effect size that you see in some of these
results.

00:37:27.330 --> 00:37:29.609
It's bananas, but it's very intense.

00:37:29.609 --> 00:37:30.880
By accelerated TMS.

00:37:30.880 --> 00:37:36.160
What that means is you are getting your brain
zapped, and it's not technically — I mean,

00:37:36.160 --> 00:37:37.160
whatever.

00:37:37.160 --> 00:37:38.320
We don't have to get into zapping.

00:37:38.320 --> 00:37:41.240
It's using magnetism, but you fucking feel
it.

00:37:41.240 --> 00:37:43.690
Let me make it very clear.

00:37:43.690 --> 00:37:49.130
You are getting zapped for, and I could be
getting some of the specifics wrong, but I've

00:37:49.130 --> 00:37:54.060
gone through one cycle of this already.

00:37:54.060 --> 00:37:59.490
You're getting zapped for something like eight
and a half to 10 minutes every hour, on the

00:37:59.490 --> 00:38:05.300
hour, for 10 hours a day, for five days straight.

00:38:05.300 --> 00:38:11.280
So you're doing 50 sessions in five days,
and it's a hell of a thing.

00:38:11.280 --> 00:38:18.359
But that is why I was undergoing a psychiatric
evaluation, was to determine what the diagnosis

00:38:18.359 --> 00:38:24.680
was, such that they could try to determine
which coordinates to use, basically, in placement

00:38:24.680 --> 00:38:26.180
of the stimulation.

00:38:26.180 --> 00:38:29.329
Super, super, super, super interesting.

00:38:29.329 --> 00:38:36.550
But the reason I brought that up is, winding
all the way back to kind of where we started,

00:38:36.550 --> 00:38:43.130
I notice really — I notice really minute
details, and that means also every mistake.

00:38:43.130 --> 00:38:48.180
I could scan document, I don't know if you're
like this, but I can look at a document that

00:38:48.180 --> 00:38:54.800
four lawyers have reviewed and find stuff,
and I just have that ability.

00:38:54.800 --> 00:38:56.480
I don't know what it is.

00:38:56.480 --> 00:38:58.430
It's definitely not all blessing.

00:38:58.430 --> 00:38:59.540
There's a lot of curse to that.

00:38:59.540 --> 00:39:05.430
But it's like I can scan a document, reading
very quickly and be like, "This clause is

00:39:05.430 --> 00:39:06.430
off.

00:39:06.430 --> 00:39:08.420
That punctuation is off," in minutes.

00:39:08.420 --> 00:39:10.760
Kevin Rose: Like a living ChatGPT.

00:39:10.760 --> 00:39:14.530
Tim Ferriss: I'm kind of like, well, yeah,
or a Rain Man in some respects.

00:39:14.530 --> 00:39:22.760
But that comes with a huge tax because it
drives other people crazy and you drive yourself

00:39:22.760 --> 00:39:24.190
crazy.

00:39:24.190 --> 00:39:31.490
And so I'm learning to contend with that,
which is a way of emphasizing how big a deal

00:39:31.490 --> 00:39:37.320
it is for me to collaborate in the way that
I'm describing.

00:39:37.320 --> 00:39:48.000
I will say also that the objective for the
creative offsite for me, was to try to create

00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:52.560
a flywheel of collaborative potential, and
I'll explain what that means.

00:39:52.560 --> 00:40:01.040
In other words, I wanted to create enough
imagery, enough artwork, also enough scaffolding

00:40:01.040 --> 00:40:06.990
in terms of world building, so the mythologies,
the beliefs, the alliances, the conflicts,

00:40:06.990 --> 00:40:15.450
the geography, such that I could take all
of that and give it as a world bible of sorts

00:40:15.450 --> 00:40:20.660
to a writer who's never had any exposure to
the realm of Varlata, right?

00:40:20.660 --> 00:40:30.250
The Legend of CØCKPUNCH, and they would be
equipped to write a short story, or a module,

00:40:30.250 --> 00:40:37.000
or an adventure for D&amp;D, a campaign, who knows,
or a comic book, and have it align with my

00:40:37.000 --> 00:40:44.700
creative vision for that entire fantasy world,
which was a cool challenge.

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:48.020
I think it requires a little more time.

00:40:48.020 --> 00:40:51.240
Those types of creative pushes are often two
to three weeks.

00:40:51.240 --> 00:40:53.330
Three weeks seems to be the sweet spot.

00:40:53.330 --> 00:40:58.200
That's a huge ask of my time and of everyone's
time, and I wanted to make sure that I felt

00:40:58.200 --> 00:41:01.130
comfortable in a shorter format first.

00:41:01.130 --> 00:41:08.770
But man, I'm excited, because what a lot of
people don't realize is this whole Legend

00:41:08.770 --> 00:41:14.070
of CØCKPUNCH thing, the only thing I need
to do to make this something serious is change

00:41:14.070 --> 00:41:15.070
the name.

00:41:15.070 --> 00:41:16.180
I don't need to change anything about the
world.

00:41:16.180 --> 00:41:17.180
Kevin Rose: Yes, I told you that.

00:41:17.180 --> 00:41:18.180
Yes.

00:41:18.180 --> 00:41:19.180
Tim Ferriss: It's actually really easy.

00:41:19.180 --> 00:41:23.140
I think if I wanted to switch that gear, which
I think would be a terrible handicap right

00:41:23.140 --> 00:41:28.540
now, because I want to continue to collaborate,
and by having the current branding, it alleviates

00:41:28.540 --> 00:41:31.200
a lot of pressure I might apply to it.

00:41:31.200 --> 00:41:36.230
There's very little I have to do to flip a
switch and make this a —

00:41:36.230 --> 00:41:37.230
Kevin Rose: Will you do that?

00:41:37.230 --> 00:41:39.190
Tim Ferriss: Extremely viable — I don't
know.

00:41:39.190 --> 00:41:40.190
I don't know.

00:41:40.190 --> 00:41:41.190
Maybe.

00:41:41.190 --> 00:41:42.190
Kevin Rose: I think you should.

00:41:42.190 --> 00:41:43.190
Tim Ferriss: Remember when we had this conversation?

00:41:43.190 --> 00:41:45.250
Kevin Rose: I called you up and I said, "If
you do these three things, this shit's going

00:41:45.250 --> 00:41:46.250
to take off."

00:41:46.250 --> 00:41:48.040
And one of them was the name change.

00:41:48.040 --> 00:41:49.510
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, of course, of course.

00:41:49.510 --> 00:41:51.670
I know it's a possibility.

00:41:51.670 --> 00:42:04.130
Right now, it is working so well as a creative
hyperdrive and as a catalyst for just — it's

00:42:04.130 --> 00:42:06.770
like having my own fusion reactor or something.

00:42:06.770 --> 00:42:11.089
It's working so well that I'm like, "Look,
don't get too clever."

00:42:11.089 --> 00:42:13.250
You can fuck things up by being too clever.

00:42:13.250 --> 00:42:15.440
I was like, "Don't get too clever.

00:42:15.440 --> 00:42:16.980
This is working really well.

00:42:16.980 --> 00:42:18.180
You've lucked into a lot of it.

00:42:18.180 --> 00:42:20.980
Some of it's by design, but you got really
lucky.

00:42:20.980 --> 00:42:23.350
Ride the lightning for a while, don't fuck
it up."

00:42:23.350 --> 00:42:25.580
So I'm going to stick with it for a while.

00:42:25.580 --> 00:42:27.660
I don't feel also any time pressure whatsoever.

00:42:27.660 --> 00:42:32.599
Kevin Rose: Can I ask you a question that
I hope you'll give me a more kind of vulnerable

00:42:32.599 --> 00:42:33.690
response to?

00:42:33.690 --> 00:42:35.349
Tim Ferriss: Oh, boy.

00:42:35.349 --> 00:42:42.059
Kevin Rose: So, no, this is a good one though,
because from an outsider looking in, you say,

00:42:42.059 --> 00:42:46.099
"Okay, Tim, you've told us," this is just
my observations.

00:42:46.099 --> 00:42:48.339
You say over and over, "Legend of CØCKPUNCH.

00:42:48.339 --> 00:42:49.339
It's a joke.

00:42:49.339 --> 00:42:50.339
It's for fun.

00:42:50.339 --> 00:42:51.339
It's a throwaway.

00:42:51.339 --> 00:42:52.339
I'm having a good time.

00:42:52.339 --> 00:42:55.000
Don't want to take it too seriously, blah,
blah, blah."

00:42:55.000 --> 00:43:00.340
But in some sense, let me just think about
how to word this.

00:43:00.340 --> 00:43:05.670
In some sense, if you did take it seriously,
now you're a, not a sci-fi, but you're a fiction

00:43:05.670 --> 00:43:07.819
writer —
Tim Ferriss: A fantasy writer.

00:43:07.819 --> 00:43:09.510
Kevin Rose: Fantasy writer.

00:43:09.510 --> 00:43:11.331
And you could go for it.

00:43:11.331 --> 00:43:16.240
You could decide at some point to go for it
and say, "I want to try this."

00:43:16.240 --> 00:43:20.510
But there's also a sense that it could fail,
and you're protected right now from failure

00:43:20.510 --> 00:43:22.599
because of the name.

00:43:22.599 --> 00:43:29.690
So is that just a safety mechanism to keep
you from feeling failure, or — do you see

00:43:29.690 --> 00:43:30.690
what I'm saying?

00:43:30.690 --> 00:43:31.730
If we're being really vulnerable here?

00:43:31.730 --> 00:43:32.730
Tim Ferriss: I do.

00:43:32.730 --> 00:43:34.720
Yeah, it's a great question.

00:43:34.720 --> 00:43:37.890
So here's what I would say.

00:43:37.890 --> 00:43:41.080
It's actually in a sense the opposite for
me.

00:43:41.080 --> 00:43:47.099
So it's not a throwaway, it's definitely not
a throwaway.

00:43:47.099 --> 00:43:56.839
It is something I think, very profound, at
least for me personally, that is in the guise

00:43:56.839 --> 00:43:58.050
of something ridiculous.

00:43:58.050 --> 00:44:05.550
Which I think more people should try, honestly,
because it's actually a cheat code.

00:44:05.550 --> 00:44:11.470
And I would say that instead of using it to
avoid the possibility of failure, what it

00:44:11.470 --> 00:44:20.380
is enabling me to do is fucking swing for
the fences in ways that I would never dare

00:44:20.380 --> 00:44:25.140
otherwise, that could result in just a complete
face plant.

00:44:25.140 --> 00:44:33.820
So I'm actually risking many, many, many different
types of failure that I would be hugely averse

00:44:33.820 --> 00:44:41.630
to otherwise, by couching it in the terms
that I've been using, and I am going to do

00:44:41.630 --> 00:44:43.460
so much more of this in life.

00:44:43.460 --> 00:44:53.470
Holy shit, this has been such an unlock for
me, that I'm going to do a lot more of it.

00:44:53.470 --> 00:45:00.480
And I'll mention a few other things just quickly
because it ties into this.

00:45:00.480 --> 00:45:01.910
That's the nature of The Random Show.

00:45:01.910 --> 00:45:07.589
Let me just pull out a random piece of paper
from the hat and we'll go from there.

00:45:07.589 --> 00:45:12.450
So there's a great book, it's actually excellent
for storytelling in general, although it is

00:45:12.450 --> 00:45:13.770
comic specific.

00:45:13.770 --> 00:45:23.060
It was recommended to me by a very good artist
and creative who works in comics named Daniel

00:45:23.060 --> 00:45:31.410
Henriques, The DC Comics Guide to Writing
Comics, which is such a boring title for a

00:45:31.410 --> 00:45:34.160
really entertaining and useful book.

00:45:34.160 --> 00:45:40.150
But The DC Comics Guide to Writing Comics
by Dennis O'Neil, it has sample scripts, it

00:45:40.150 --> 00:45:43.460
shows you side by side script plus final output.

00:45:43.460 --> 00:45:46.490
It goes into story arcs.

00:45:46.490 --> 00:45:52.690
Not all of it would apply to what I want to
do, but it was — I found it incredibly useful

00:45:52.690 --> 00:45:54.369
and also hilarious.

00:45:54.369 --> 00:45:55.710
The writing itself is spectacular.

00:45:55.710 --> 00:45:57.460
I thought it was spectacular.

00:45:57.460 --> 00:45:58.460
By Dennis O'Neil.

00:45:58.460 --> 00:46:01.250
So I want to give a hats off to him.

00:46:01.250 --> 00:46:13.180
And in terms of fiction, I would be very insecure
if I were to take fiction writing, prose only

00:46:13.180 --> 00:46:21.730
on, in, say, novel or book form, because there
are some people who are so, so good at it.

00:46:21.730 --> 00:46:26.490
For people out there who have not read any,
say fantasy fiction, we could certainly get

00:46:26.490 --> 00:46:27.490
into science fiction.

00:46:27.490 --> 00:46:29.960
I'm a huge fan of science fiction.

00:46:29.960 --> 00:46:30.960
Ted Chiang.

00:46:30.960 --> 00:46:32.140
I'll just say that.

00:46:32.140 --> 00:46:33.140
C-H-I-A-N-G.

00:46:33.140 --> 00:46:35.170
The collection's just short stories if you
want something —

00:46:35.170 --> 00:46:37.920
Kevin Rose: Oh, it's got like eight short
stories or something like that in one book?

00:46:37.920 --> 00:46:38.920
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, exactly.

00:46:38.920 --> 00:46:41.210
Kevin Rose: That was really good.

00:46:41.210 --> 00:46:43.330
You said that a couple of years ago, you recommended
that.

00:46:43.330 --> 00:46:44.330
I read it.

00:46:44.330 --> 00:46:45.330
Fantastic.

00:46:45.330 --> 00:46:46.330
Tim Ferriss: I mean, he's absurdly, absurdly
good.

00:46:46.330 --> 00:46:47.330
The movie Arrival —
Kevin Rose: So good.

00:46:47.330 --> 00:46:48.920
Tim Ferriss: Was based on one of his short
stories.

00:46:48.920 --> 00:46:50.660
I mean, the guy is so, so fantastic.

00:46:50.660 --> 00:46:55.930
Kevin Rose: To be able to pack that much information,
like that dense of storytelling, in such a

00:46:55.930 --> 00:46:59.869
short little — it brings you in so quickly.

00:46:59.869 --> 00:47:00.869
It's unbelievable.

00:47:00.869 --> 00:47:06.940
Tim Ferriss: His ability to turn your brain
inside out with new concepts in a short story

00:47:06.940 --> 00:47:09.170
is —
Kevin Rose: In 15 pages.

00:47:09.170 --> 00:47:10.290
Tim Ferriss: Unbelievable.

00:47:10.290 --> 00:47:11.290
It's unbelievable.

00:47:11.290 --> 00:47:13.940
So Ted Chiang for science fiction would be
my starting point.

00:47:13.940 --> 00:47:16.210
And then you can try the longer stuff.

00:47:16.210 --> 00:47:22.319
But for fantasy, if people — Lord of the
Rings is just too much for people to chew

00:47:22.319 --> 00:47:23.849
on, generally.

00:47:23.849 --> 00:47:26.550
The Name of the Wind by Patrick Rothfuss.

00:47:26.550 --> 00:47:29.359
I think Darya has read that actually, because
I recommended it —

00:47:29.359 --> 00:47:30.359
Kevin Rose: She's read almost all sci-fi.

00:47:30.359 --> 00:47:32.119
Tim Ferriss: She read The Graveyard Book,
which was usually my — The Graveyard Book

00:47:32.119 --> 00:47:37.890
by Neil Gaiman is usually my, what would you
call it?

00:47:37.890 --> 00:47:42.150
My trainer for people who are non-fiction
purists who are like, "Fiction is dumb.

00:47:42.150 --> 00:47:43.170
I don't want to read fiction."

00:47:43.170 --> 00:47:47.619
I'll usually break them in with The Graveyard
Book by Neil Gaiman.

00:47:47.619 --> 00:47:50.869
Usually audiobook, because these people are
like, "I'm fucking busy."

00:47:50.869 --> 00:47:51.869
I'm like, "Okay, fine.

00:47:51.869 --> 00:47:54.470
Well, listen while you're walking the dog
or doing the dishes or whatever."

00:47:54.470 --> 00:47:59.329
The Graveyard Book, read by Neil Gaiman — the
ensemble is great, but don't do the ensemble

00:47:59.329 --> 00:48:00.890
cast — read by Neil Gaiman.

00:48:00.890 --> 00:48:05.800
And then the other one is The Name of The
Wind by Patrick Rothfuss, which is fucking

00:48:05.800 --> 00:48:06.810
incredible.

00:48:06.810 --> 00:48:07.930
And I recommended it to a friend.

00:48:07.930 --> 00:48:09.930
I hadn't read it in 10 years or something.

00:48:09.930 --> 00:48:16.020
And my friend, who is a fucking snob when
it comes to writing, he has very high standards,

00:48:16.020 --> 00:48:17.880
he reads excellent books.

00:48:17.880 --> 00:48:25.660
And he ripped through the two books that are
part of this series, The Kingkiller Chronicle,

00:48:25.660 --> 00:48:27.070
in like four days.

00:48:27.070 --> 00:48:28.070
These are long books.

00:48:28.070 --> 00:48:33.980
And it completely blew his mind, which got
me very excited to read more fiction.

00:48:33.980 --> 00:48:39.859
But I didn't have a good lead until these
concept artists, at least I think one of the

00:48:39.859 --> 00:48:44.950
concept artists and one of the writers at
least, it was like three out of five or something,

00:48:44.950 --> 00:48:48.800
said, "You have to read The Blade Itself by
Joe Abercrombie."

00:48:48.800 --> 00:48:57.109
And I'm in the middle of listening to The
Blade Itself by Joe Abercrombie, and it is

00:48:57.109 --> 00:48:58.109
really fun.

00:48:58.109 --> 00:48:59.570
It is really, really, really good.

00:48:59.570 --> 00:49:01.609
I've really been enjoying it.

00:49:01.609 --> 00:49:09.500
So on the creative side, I'm soaking myself
in masters of fiction, but I don't have the

00:49:09.500 --> 00:49:12.400
balls yet, or let me put it a different way.

00:49:12.400 --> 00:49:14.349
It's not a balls issue.

00:49:14.349 --> 00:49:20.040
I don't have the hubris to want to take that
on right now.

00:49:20.040 --> 00:49:27.490
I realize I have a lot of skill development
to focus on, and I think that my sweet spot

00:49:27.490 --> 00:49:30.510
will actually be potentially comics.

00:49:30.510 --> 00:49:33.270
I really think comics might be my sweet spot.

00:49:33.270 --> 00:49:34.500
Kevin Rose: That would be awesome.

00:49:34.500 --> 00:49:36.630
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, because I have the visual
sensibility.

00:49:36.630 --> 00:49:43.549
I mean, I do have the directorial visual capacity.

00:49:43.549 --> 00:49:46.589
I can think about all of that very easily.

00:49:46.589 --> 00:49:50.310
So I could see you really, really enjoying
that.

00:49:50.310 --> 00:49:53.750
And I want to recommend one other thing, which
is there's a very short, it's not really a

00:49:53.750 --> 00:49:56.830
TED Talk, but it's on TED.

00:49:56.830 --> 00:50:00.970
There's a nine-minute monologue.

00:50:00.970 --> 00:50:06.799
And what's genius about it is that he's such
a good actor that he makes it seem impromptu,

00:50:06.799 --> 00:50:09.460
but you know he rehearsed the shit out of
it.

00:50:09.460 --> 00:50:14.430
It takes a lot of practice to make something
seem like it's impromptu.

00:50:14.430 --> 00:50:20.049
And it's Ethan Hawk, the actor, and it is
a short TED Talk.

00:50:20.049 --> 00:50:23.440
It's like nine minutes long called “Give
Yourself Permission to Be Creative.”

00:50:23.440 --> 00:50:29.330
And holy shit, it is so good and so powerful.

00:50:29.330 --> 00:50:32.010
I really, really recommend everybody check
it out.

00:50:32.010 --> 00:50:33.460
Kevin Rose: I'm writing it down right now.

00:50:33.460 --> 00:50:34.930
Tim Ferriss: It's such an easy lift.

00:50:34.930 --> 00:50:36.089
Kevin Rose: Amazing.

00:50:36.089 --> 00:50:37.260
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:50:37.260 --> 00:50:40.359
Speaking of TED Talks, thank you for coming
to my TED Talk.

00:50:40.359 --> 00:50:41.359
Kevin, you're up.

00:50:41.359 --> 00:50:45.780
Kevin Rose: Yeah, so one thing I wanted to
— we talked about that brain stimulation

00:50:45.780 --> 00:50:50.702
stuff, and Adam Gazzaley, our mutual friend
at UCSF, has been experimenting with some

00:50:50.702 --> 00:50:51.702
of that stuff as well.

00:50:51.702 --> 00:50:52.702
Tim Ferriss: For a long time.

00:50:52.702 --> 00:50:53.702
For a long time.

00:50:53.702 --> 00:50:54.702
He's one of the pros.

00:50:54.702 --> 00:50:56.280
Kevin Rose: Long time.

00:50:56.280 --> 00:51:02.180
One thing that I will say, our mutual friend,
Matt Walker, who runs the Berkeley Sleep Lab,

00:51:02.180 --> 00:51:04.319
have you talked to Matt about brain stimulation
at all?

00:51:04.319 --> 00:51:05.319
Tim Ferriss: No.

00:51:05.319 --> 00:51:09.940
You know, God, what a fucking — I'm really
on my Long Island self right now.

00:51:09.940 --> 00:51:12.630
My F-bombs are at a high density for this
episode.

00:51:12.630 --> 00:51:18.980
I literally took a note like 10 minutes ago
to catch up with Matt Walker, because I haven't

00:51:18.980 --> 00:51:20.109
caught up with him in a while.

00:51:20.109 --> 00:51:21.609
So yes, I've been thinking about that.

00:51:21.609 --> 00:51:22.609
Kevin Rose: Probably one of the nicest nicest
humans on Earth.

00:51:22.609 --> 00:51:25.960
Tim Ferriss: Such a sweet guy, but I haven't
talked to him about brain stim.

00:51:25.960 --> 00:51:27.020
I have not.

00:51:27.020 --> 00:51:32.630
Kevin Rose: Matt is, and this is by no means
an ad, I'm not involved at all, he's a scientific

00:51:32.630 --> 00:51:38.980
advisor for a company called Somnee, S-O-M-N-E-E.

00:51:38.980 --> 00:51:40.190
He sent me one of the devices.

00:51:40.190 --> 00:51:45.610
I haven't tried it yet, but I have it sitting
next to my bed, oddly enough, as one does.

00:51:45.610 --> 00:51:50.369
But you wear it 15 minutes before you go to
sleep, and it's supposed to just really improve

00:51:50.369 --> 00:51:54.240
your sleep quality through, via brain stimulation.

00:51:54.240 --> 00:51:57.470
So definitely check that out.

00:51:57.470 --> 00:52:01.100
And I know that obviously he wouldn't sign
on to something unless he believed the science

00:52:01.100 --> 00:52:05.450
was solid, and he was very excited about this.

00:52:05.450 --> 00:52:12.590
It's a tough hurdle as a startup to get people
to feel comfortable stimulating their brain

00:52:12.590 --> 00:52:14.240
with currents.

00:52:14.240 --> 00:52:16.720
It's challenging, but it's really promising.

00:52:16.720 --> 00:52:18.569
And Matt was impressed with the result.

00:52:18.569 --> 00:52:19.569
Tim Ferriss: That's amazing.

00:52:19.569 --> 00:52:21.680
Kevin Rose: So if you have trouble with sleep,
definitely check it out.

00:52:21.680 --> 00:52:23.190
And Matt's obviously a legend.

00:52:23.190 --> 00:52:24.190
Tim Ferriss: Oh, my God.

00:52:24.190 --> 00:52:25.190
Story of my life.

00:52:25.190 --> 00:52:26.300
Good lord.

00:52:26.300 --> 00:52:27.300
Yeah.

00:52:27.300 --> 00:52:28.660
So I will talk to Matt.

00:52:28.660 --> 00:52:35.630
He is genuinely such a brilliant, but moreover,
sweet guy.

00:52:35.630 --> 00:52:38.250
He's just one of the sweetest guys I've ever
met.

00:52:38.250 --> 00:52:39.250
Kevin Rose: Oh, he's nice.

00:52:39.250 --> 00:52:42.770
I kind of want to write him into my will and
have him watch my kids if I die.

00:52:42.770 --> 00:52:44.559
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, seriously.

00:52:44.559 --> 00:52:49.680
"I know I met you a year ago, but can you
be the godfather to my unborn children, please?"

00:52:49.680 --> 00:52:51.840
Kevin Rose: "You've never met them, but…"

00:52:51.840 --> 00:52:53.510
Tim Ferriss: Exactly.

00:52:53.510 --> 00:52:54.880
He is the sweetest guy.

00:52:54.880 --> 00:52:55.960
Such a sweet guy.

00:52:55.960 --> 00:52:58.700
All right, so I know you have a bunch of stuff.

00:52:58.700 --> 00:53:00.840
What else are you holding back here?

00:53:00.840 --> 00:53:09.690
Kevin Rose: Speaking of just crazy shit early
that I want to tell you about, I think about,

00:53:09.690 --> 00:53:14.930
well, technology startups in general, we all
know 90 percent of them fail.

00:53:14.930 --> 00:53:22.240
Some of them are so odd they're worth mentioning
because if they become something larger, you'll

00:53:22.240 --> 00:53:24.599
want to have heard about it.

00:53:24.599 --> 00:53:26.190
This is one of those.

00:53:26.190 --> 00:53:32.180
So there is a site out there, it is called
friend.tech.

00:53:32.180 --> 00:53:33.230
Have you heard of this?

00:53:33.230 --> 00:53:34.230
Tim Ferriss: No.

00:53:34.230 --> 00:53:35.230
Kevin Rose: Okay.

00:53:35.230 --> 00:53:36.230
Tim Ferriss: I don't think so.

00:53:36.230 --> 00:53:40.790
Kevin Rose: So you get a signup code, they're
posting them all over Twitter, somebody you'll

00:53:40.790 --> 00:53:42.210
know will have one.

00:53:42.210 --> 00:53:49.590
And when you go in, what they have realized,
and what Apple has done, is Apple has said,

00:53:49.590 --> 00:53:53.680
"You must pay us a 20 percent tax on all transactions."

00:53:53.680 --> 00:53:59.220
And they've also banned cryptocurrency trading
from the App Store.

00:53:59.220 --> 00:54:00.530
They don't allow that to happen.

00:54:00.530 --> 00:54:06.520
They like to have wallets and they allow you
to buy from Coinbase, but as payments, they

00:54:06.520 --> 00:54:08.890
haven't yet enabled that.

00:54:08.890 --> 00:54:14.810
So some clever individuals have figured out
that they, back in the day, they have a way

00:54:14.810 --> 00:54:21.720
with Safari, the little browser built into
the iPhone, to install apps on the phone that

00:54:21.720 --> 00:54:23.250
aren't actually from the App Store.

00:54:23.250 --> 00:54:24.520
Tim Ferriss: That's wild.

00:54:24.520 --> 00:54:25.520
Okay.

00:54:25.520 --> 00:54:26.810
I see where this is going.

00:54:26.810 --> 00:54:27.810
Kevin Rose: Okay.

00:54:27.810 --> 00:54:33.170
So what they've done is, somebody created
something called friend.tech.

00:54:33.170 --> 00:54:36.910
And when you sign in with your Twitter and
off with your Twitter, it creates a profile

00:54:36.910 --> 00:54:37.910
for you.

00:54:37.910 --> 00:54:40.299
It gives you a place to post to your followers.

00:54:40.299 --> 00:54:45.200
You can post out and they can all respond
back, but they can't see each other's responses.

00:54:45.200 --> 00:54:49.020
So it's almost like a private DM that is coming
from them to you.

