01:06:35.890 --> 01:06:37.630
Tim Ferriss: Some do.

01:06:37.630 --> 01:06:38.630
Some do.

01:06:38.630 --> 01:06:45.329
In psilocybe mushrooms or different types
of mushrooms, so magic mushrooms, in Parkinson's

01:06:45.329 --> 01:06:53.250
patients for the minimizing or reversal of
symptoms.

01:06:53.250 --> 01:06:56.270
And it's very compelling.

01:06:56.270 --> 01:07:04.520
So I do think that psychedelics, it's unclear
exactly what type, to me at least, maybe they

01:07:04.520 --> 01:07:10.490
are the tryptamine psychedelics.

01:07:10.490 --> 01:07:11.490
Who knows?

01:07:11.490 --> 01:07:13.700
Maybe it has something to do with the serotonin
type 2A receptors.

01:07:13.700 --> 01:07:14.700
I'm not sure.

01:07:14.700 --> 01:07:15.700
That's beyond my pay grade.

01:07:15.700 --> 01:07:24.100
But I will say that, for those who are interested,
if they can find this research, which could

01:07:24.100 --> 01:07:25.940
be very preliminary, who knows?

01:07:25.940 --> 01:07:30.430
Maybe it's kind of pilot study type of status.

01:07:30.430 --> 01:07:35.700
But looking at psilocybin as applied to Parkinson's
patients.

01:07:35.700 --> 01:07:45.460
I do think, from a neurogenesis perspective,
I find it one of the more promising avenues

01:07:45.460 --> 01:07:48.420
of exploration.

01:07:48.420 --> 01:07:55.420
For people with TBI, I do think psychedelic-assisted
therapies are very interesting.

01:07:55.420 --> 01:08:02.750
I would say psilocybin is probably one of
the better-researched molecules and worth

01:08:02.750 --> 01:08:03.750
investigating.

01:08:03.750 --> 01:08:05.390
Ibogaine, also very interesting.

01:08:05.390 --> 01:08:06.790
But it has cardiac implications.

01:08:06.790 --> 01:08:08.569
Kevin Rose: It stops your heart.

01:08:08.569 --> 01:08:10.349
Tim Ferriss: There are cardiac implications.

01:08:10.349 --> 01:08:18.060
Nolan Williams, I mentioned earlier, has looked
at ways to mitigate some of the cardiac risk.

01:08:18.060 --> 01:08:20.520
Both of those are very, very, very interesting.

01:08:20.520 --> 01:08:25.890
Psilocybin, at this point, probably being
the more versatile of the two just from a

01:08:25.890 --> 01:08:27.960
safety profile perspective.

01:08:27.960 --> 01:08:33.159
But I'm very optimistic.

01:08:33.159 --> 01:08:40.380
For those people who want to look at the edges
of scientific inquiry, I'm very optimistic

01:08:40.380 --> 01:08:50.969
actually, in a way that I wasn't, say, 10
years ago in how we might contend with neurocognitive

01:08:50.969 --> 01:08:53.569
decline or neurodegenerative disease.

01:08:53.569 --> 01:08:57.839
I'm actually much more optimistic than I was
10 years ago.

01:08:57.839 --> 01:09:00.839
Kevin Rose: Did you happen to invest in Paul
Stamets' new company?

01:09:00.839 --> 01:09:03.179
Tim Ferriss: I did not.

01:09:03.179 --> 01:09:05.230
I'm not doing a whole lot of investing and
I haven't.

01:09:05.230 --> 01:09:06.330
Kevin Rose: We did at True Ventures.

01:09:06.330 --> 01:09:07.330
Tim Ferriss: You did?

01:09:07.330 --> 01:09:08.330
Good.

01:09:08.330 --> 01:09:11.409
Kevin Rose: He's got a psilocybin new company
that they're doing studies.

01:09:11.409 --> 01:09:14.549
And hopefully, when this stuff gets legalized,
we'll see what happens.

01:09:14.549 --> 01:09:18.319
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, Paul Stamets knows this
stuff, man.

01:09:18.319 --> 01:09:20.830
Paul Stamets, he's one of a kind.

01:09:20.830 --> 01:09:24.929
He's a great human being in my experience,
all my experiences with him.

01:09:24.929 --> 01:09:30.469
We actually, just last week, maybe in the
last two weeks, he and I did a challenge grant

01:09:30.469 --> 01:09:39.449
to raise money for the Amazon conservation
team to buy a historically indigenous land

01:09:39.449 --> 01:09:45.529
to return it to certain tribes in Colombia,
including the Kogi and a handful of others.

01:09:45.529 --> 01:09:51.180
They're not in the Amazon, they're at higher
altitude in the north, and a handful of others.

01:09:51.180 --> 01:09:56.820
And then ACT equips them with training to
be able to sort of monitor and report on their

01:09:56.820 --> 01:10:00.570
territory, because it's not really enough
just to secure land rights.

01:10:00.570 --> 01:10:08.260
And Paul was generous enough, along with my
foundation, to issue the challenge grant.

01:10:08.260 --> 01:10:11.000
So we just did that together in the last two
weeks.

01:10:11.000 --> 01:10:12.659
Paul knows his stuff.

01:10:12.659 --> 01:10:14.760
He really, really knows his stuff.

01:10:14.760 --> 01:10:16.929
I feel very confident in saying that.

01:10:16.929 --> 01:10:17.929
Smart guy.

01:10:17.929 --> 01:10:21.460
Kevin Rose: He's got some great TED talks
as well, worth checking out.

01:10:21.460 --> 01:10:24.230
All right, should we move on?

01:10:24.230 --> 01:10:28.850
Tim Ferriss: Let's move on.

01:10:28.850 --> 01:10:35.190
So I'll mention something that's sitting outside,
I can see it from here, that people might

01:10:35.190 --> 01:10:36.190
be interested in.

01:10:36.190 --> 01:10:43.739
So I've been and still am a fervent believer
in cold therapy.

01:10:43.739 --> 01:10:52.989
So using ice baths as a means of controlling
inflammation, as a means of mood elevation

01:10:52.989 --> 01:10:56.270
that lasts —
Kevin Rose: Yeah, mood elevation, big time.

01:10:56.270 --> 01:10:57.719
Tim Ferriss: It's unbelievable.

01:10:57.719 --> 01:11:04.180
And generally, my approach has been to get
a chest freezer of some type and fill it with

01:11:04.180 --> 01:11:05.180
ice, et cetera.

01:11:05.180 --> 01:11:08.490
And that works, but they get really disgusting
really quickly.

01:11:08.490 --> 01:11:09.590
Kevin Rose: They all do, dude.

01:11:09.590 --> 01:11:11.690
I bought one of the machines, the real pro
ones.

01:11:11.690 --> 01:11:12.690
Tim Ferriss: Yes.

01:11:12.690 --> 01:11:17.190
And then you're stuck with this thing that
is an eyesore and you don't know what to do

01:11:17.190 --> 01:11:18.190
with it.

01:11:18.190 --> 01:11:19.190
Kevin Rose: You can't move it.

01:11:19.190 --> 01:11:20.190
I tried to sell it.

01:11:20.190 --> 01:11:21.190
I couldn't.

01:11:21.190 --> 01:11:29.150
Tim Ferriss: So I ended up, because I realized
how significant a lever this is for improving

01:11:29.150 --> 01:11:34.050
my quality of life just on a daily basis — and
I'll backstep for a second and just say one

01:11:34.050 --> 01:11:38.660
thing that I learned from Tony Robbins, to
give him credit, and I don't know if he came

01:11:38.660 --> 01:11:45.020
up with this, but is the sequence of state,
story, strategy.

01:11:45.020 --> 01:11:49.750
So it's like, fix your state first.

01:11:49.750 --> 01:11:53.960
Only then can you come up with an enabling
story, and only then can you come up with

01:11:53.960 --> 01:11:56.050
an effective strategy.

01:11:56.050 --> 01:11:59.460
Because if you're feeling shitty and you're
sleep-deprived and you're kind of depressed

01:11:59.460 --> 01:12:03.550
or anxious or whatever, you try to come up
with a strategy, meaning, how to fix something,

01:12:03.550 --> 01:12:06.469
you're not going to come up with a good strategy,
generally speaking.

01:12:06.469 --> 01:12:15.590
So it's like, fix your physical state first,
then you can come up with an enabling story,

01:12:15.590 --> 01:12:16.949
then come up with an effective strategy.

01:12:16.949 --> 01:12:19.330
So the state piece is very important.

01:12:19.330 --> 01:12:21.889
There aren't that many ways I have found to
change state.

01:12:21.889 --> 01:12:26.560
Exercise is one, but it requires generally
a fair amount of time.

01:12:26.560 --> 01:12:33.840
Heat is also effective, but again, generally
requires, say, it doesn't sound like much,

01:12:33.840 --> 01:12:36.280
but it's like 15 to 30 minutes, let's just
say, in a sauna.

01:12:36.280 --> 01:12:37.670
It's very effective.

01:12:37.670 --> 01:12:39.100
Cold is the fastest.

01:12:39.100 --> 01:12:40.929
It's just the fastest for me.

01:12:40.929 --> 01:12:46.659
And I've gone without cold therapy because
I've not wanted to get some huge chest freezer

01:12:46.659 --> 01:12:48.800
or a pro model.

01:12:48.800 --> 01:12:50.620
I am also going to be getting a pro model.

01:12:50.620 --> 01:12:54.290
Kevin Rose: They get all scummy though, and
then you've got to put chlorine in them and

01:12:54.290 --> 01:12:55.290
it's a lot.

01:12:55.290 --> 01:13:01.510
Tim Ferriss: But I went on Amazon and I found
something called the Cold Pod, which you set

01:13:01.510 --> 01:13:03.810
up in like five minutes.

01:13:03.810 --> 01:13:10.230
You fill it with water from a hose, you put
ice in and it works perfectly well as a cold

01:13:10.230 --> 01:13:11.230
plunge.

01:13:11.230 --> 01:13:12.230
Kevin Rose: Is it like a little body wrap?

01:13:12.230 --> 01:13:15.989
Tim Ferriss: And I ended up buying a Yeti
cooler to store ice in.

01:13:15.989 --> 01:13:18.310
So I have a few days of ice.

01:13:18.310 --> 01:13:21.989
And it's, let's see, what would it be?

01:13:21.989 --> 01:13:31.170
It would be like if you were standing, maybe
it's hip height and it's probably three feet

01:13:31.170 --> 01:13:32.170
in diameter.

01:13:32.170 --> 01:13:36.679
And you get in, you just kind of crouch in
there and it works fucking great.

01:13:36.679 --> 01:13:41.130
It works really well — and it costs like
150 or 200 bucks maybe.

01:13:41.130 --> 01:13:42.130
I don't know.

01:13:42.130 --> 01:13:43.130
Maybe it's a hundred bucks.

01:13:43.130 --> 01:13:44.130
It's not crazy.

01:13:44.130 --> 01:13:45.130
Kevin Rose: 169.

01:13:45.130 --> 01:13:46.610
Tim Ferriss: There you go.

01:13:46.610 --> 01:13:49.429
And I've been really impressed with this thing.

01:13:49.429 --> 01:13:51.070
It's very basic.

01:13:51.070 --> 01:13:56.449
And even when it's been very hot outside,
you put in two bags of ice and you let it

01:13:56.449 --> 01:13:58.739
sit for 20 minutes, it's going to get pretty
cold.

01:13:58.739 --> 01:14:02.489
It's not going to be 30 degrees, but it's
probably going to be mid-fifties, depending

01:14:02.489 --> 01:14:04.110
on how much water you have in there.

01:14:04.110 --> 01:14:07.739
Pro tip, the more water you have in there,
the more ice you're going to need to cool

01:14:07.739 --> 01:14:08.739
it.

01:14:08.739 --> 01:14:14.710
So don't fill it all the way up to the top,
fill it up like halfway and then add the ice.

01:14:14.710 --> 01:14:16.330
And it's been a game changer for me.

01:14:16.330 --> 01:14:18.320
Kevin Rose: This is a plastic trash can, dude.

01:14:18.320 --> 01:14:20.900
Tim Ferriss: Well, no, it's not a plastic
trash can.

01:14:20.900 --> 01:14:23.429
Kevin Rose: I mean, props to whoever made
this shit because it probably cost them like

01:14:23.429 --> 01:14:26.080
15 bucks to build one of these things.

01:14:26.080 --> 01:14:29.750
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it's not a trash can, just
to be clear.

01:14:29.750 --> 01:14:33.020
Look, we're not talking about a fucking Maserati.

01:14:33.020 --> 01:14:34.250
But here's what's nice about it.

01:14:34.250 --> 01:14:35.600
It's very easy to set up.

01:14:35.600 --> 01:14:41.240
It is not a trash can because that makes it
sound like it's like mid-chest height and

01:14:41.240 --> 01:14:43.320
hard.

01:14:43.320 --> 01:14:44.540
It's easy to pack.

01:14:44.540 --> 01:14:48.250
You can actually travel with it, if you want
to travel with it.

01:14:48.250 --> 01:14:50.550
I would just say with people who have —
Kevin Rose: How do you travel with it?

01:14:50.550 --> 01:14:52.300
Tim Ferriss: Oh, it's super easy, man.

01:14:52.300 --> 01:14:53.300
Kevin Rose: Oh, it folds up?

01:14:53.300 --> 01:14:54.300
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it folds up.

01:14:54.300 --> 01:14:56.260
You can stick it in the bag and take it with
you.

01:14:56.260 --> 01:14:57.260
It's super easy.

01:14:57.260 --> 01:14:58.390
Kevin Rose: To your hotel room.

01:14:58.390 --> 01:15:03.980
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, the hotel loves 50 gallons
of water spilling out.

01:15:03.980 --> 01:15:10.409
So for those people who have perhaps heard
about the benefits of cold therapy but have

01:15:10.409 --> 01:15:15.790
avoided it because it's too expensive, it's
too time-consuming, whatever.

01:15:15.790 --> 01:15:17.610
This is a way to test it.

01:15:17.610 --> 01:15:21.190
And I would just say this is a low hurdle
way to test it out.

01:15:21.190 --> 01:15:25.840
And I used it earlier today, it’s fantastic,
changed my day.

01:15:25.840 --> 01:15:27.370
So that's the Cold Pod.

01:15:27.370 --> 01:15:30.970
And there are a bunch of other options that
look basically identical.

01:15:30.970 --> 01:15:32.969
I just went with the one with the best reviews.

01:15:32.969 --> 01:15:34.350
Nothing fancy.

01:15:34.350 --> 01:15:40.030
Kevin Rose: I need something like this because
I don't want to commit to sterilizing my water.

01:15:40.030 --> 01:15:42.179
That's always been the problem with these
things.

01:15:42.179 --> 01:15:44.190
I just want something I can get into.

01:15:44.190 --> 01:15:46.790
But how many bags of ice do you really need
to pull this off?

01:15:46.790 --> 01:15:50.889
Tim Ferriss: I'd have to go look at the bags
that are in the Yeti right now.

01:15:50.889 --> 01:15:53.449
I mean you need —
Kevin Rose: Like six?

01:15:53.449 --> 01:15:54.449
Tim Ferriss: No.

01:15:54.449 --> 01:15:56.830
Well, it depends on the size of the bag, right?

01:15:56.830 --> 01:16:03.070
I would say, I'm guessing, I have no idea
if this is accurate, two 20 pound bags of

01:16:03.070 --> 01:16:04.880
ice probably.

01:16:04.880 --> 01:16:11.090
So something like that, assuming that it is
about half full.

01:16:11.090 --> 01:16:17.159
If you fill it up to the brim, you are making
a mistake because now to lower the temperature,

01:16:17.159 --> 01:16:23.020
you're going to need a higher volume, a larger
volume of ice.

01:16:23.020 --> 01:16:27.670
But if we're talking about, let's just say
it's halfway up and then you put in two 20

01:16:27.670 --> 01:16:29.530
pound bags of ice, you're set.

01:16:29.530 --> 01:16:34.230
So with a Yeti cooler and this thing, I am
good to go.

01:16:34.230 --> 01:16:36.659
I’ve got a question for you, Kev.

01:16:36.659 --> 01:16:41.060
Have you ever read poetry?

01:16:41.060 --> 01:16:42.280
This is a hard left.

01:16:42.280 --> 01:16:43.280
Kevin Rose: Yes.

01:16:43.280 --> 01:16:44.280
Tim Ferriss: Yeah?

01:16:44.280 --> 01:16:45.280
What do you read?

01:16:45.280 --> 01:16:47.770
Kevin Rose: I shouldn't say it's poetry.

01:16:47.770 --> 01:16:48.770
Tim Ferriss: It's tweets.

01:16:48.770 --> 01:16:50.870
Kevin Rose: No, no, no.

01:16:50.870 --> 01:16:57.940
Let me tell you, I'm going to send you the
book that I have found that I started reading

01:16:57.940 --> 01:16:59.540
recently that was fantastic.

01:16:59.540 --> 01:17:00.540
But I've dabbled.

01:17:00.540 --> 01:17:01.540
Tim Ferriss: Horton Hears a Who?

01:17:01.540 --> 01:17:02.920
Kevin Rose: No, no, no.

01:17:02.920 --> 01:17:08.550
I've had a couple of books that I've picked
up, but they're mostly haiku books.

01:17:08.550 --> 01:17:10.389
For me, haiku is kind of my poetry.

01:17:10.389 --> 01:17:11.889
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, sure.

01:17:11.889 --> 01:17:13.860
It's sort of in the zone, right?

01:17:13.860 --> 01:17:15.360
Kevin Rose: It's in the zone.

01:17:15.360 --> 01:17:19.690
I like haiku because it really has to hit.

01:17:19.690 --> 01:17:22.510
Don't fuck with me.

01:17:22.510 --> 01:17:23.860
It really has to hit, though.

01:17:23.860 --> 01:17:24.860
You know what I mean?

01:17:24.860 --> 01:17:29.160
You've got three lines, you've got to pull
something pretty amazing.

01:17:29.160 --> 01:17:34.020
Speaking of that sci-fi writer, it really
forces you to have something really dense

01:17:34.020 --> 01:17:39.670
and trigger, make the mind leap in those three
lines, as they say.

01:17:39.670 --> 01:17:41.580
Tim Ferriss: It's a strong constraint.

01:17:41.580 --> 01:17:42.900
Kevin Rose: Yeah, for sure.

01:17:42.900 --> 01:17:48.840
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I was wondering because
I've been delving strongly back into poetry.

01:17:48.840 --> 01:18:00.010
And that Ethan Hawke nine-minute TED Talk,
which is very informal, but don't let it fool

01:18:00.010 --> 01:18:04.300
you, he prepared for it, the giving yourself
permission to be creative.

01:18:04.300 --> 01:18:09.409
He talks about poetry and he mentions, and
I'm going to butcher this, but he says, "No

01:18:09.409 --> 01:18:15.630
one really cares about poetry, or you wonder
why on Earth people would read poetry until

01:18:15.630 --> 01:18:16.929
you have something happen.

01:18:16.929 --> 01:18:19.520
It could be something tragic, like the death
of a parent.

01:18:19.520 --> 01:18:23.650
It could be something incredibly joyous, like
the birth of a child.

01:18:23.650 --> 01:18:27.590
And you wonder, has anyone ever felt this
way before?

01:18:27.590 --> 01:18:29.230
How am I going to get through this?

01:18:29.230 --> 01:18:32.030
Or how should I think about this?

01:18:32.030 --> 01:18:37.949
And then poetry becomes really relevant or
it can become really relevant."

01:18:37.949 --> 01:18:41.949
And I've been delving back into Mary Oliver.

01:18:41.949 --> 01:18:43.679
I read her collection, Devotions.

01:18:43.679 --> 01:18:47.510
And I recently bought a small collection called
Dream Work, which I haven't finished.

01:18:47.510 --> 01:18:52.739
So in disclosure, if you read it and you hate
it, I'm not going to take the blame for it.

01:18:52.739 --> 01:19:01.530
I also have reread now multiple times this
collection of newly translated Rumi, called

01:19:01.530 --> 01:19:04.630
Gold, by Haleh Liza Gafori.

01:19:04.630 --> 01:19:05.880
Kevin Rose: Yeah, Rumi's great.

01:19:05.880 --> 01:19:13.630
Tim Ferriss: Who is particularly interesting
to me because she is a poet herself in English,

01:19:13.630 --> 01:19:15.969
but she's also a native Farsi speaker.

01:19:15.969 --> 01:19:22.449
So she can go back to the source material,
the original, and translate effectively.

01:19:22.449 --> 01:19:31.560
And what many people don't realize is that
many versions of translated poetry are not

01:19:31.560 --> 01:19:33.590
actually directly translated.

01:19:33.590 --> 01:19:38.920
They take some earlier English translation
from the 1920s or something and then they

01:19:38.920 --> 01:19:40.440
turn it into better poetry.

01:19:40.440 --> 01:19:47.159
But now you have a leap from, say, Farsi to
English to English, which is like a game of

01:19:47.159 --> 01:19:49.200
telephone, things get distorted.

01:19:49.200 --> 01:19:53.139
Whereas she can go directly.

01:19:53.139 --> 01:19:59.750
So Gold, this very short collection of Rumi
poetry, I've reread a number of times.

01:19:59.750 --> 01:20:07.520
And this particular poet, I have not read
this yet, but you can see this, it says Time

01:20:07.520 --> 01:20:19.500
Is a Mother by Ocean Vuong, V-U-O-N-G, which
I assume is Vietnamese.

01:20:19.500 --> 01:20:26.320
I read his first book, which blew my mind.

01:20:26.320 --> 01:20:28.179
I'm not sure why I was drawn to it.

01:20:28.179 --> 01:20:33.320
I bought it as an impulse purchase at some
Barnes and Noble in New York City, and I think

01:20:33.320 --> 01:20:35.010
it was probably the title.

01:20:35.010 --> 01:20:39.050
The title was Night Sky with Exit Wounds,
which is a great fucking title.

01:20:39.050 --> 01:20:40.160
It's so good.

01:20:40.160 --> 01:20:42.470
And it had this family photo.

01:20:42.470 --> 01:20:43.470
Kevin Rose: That's a great title.

01:20:43.470 --> 01:20:44.470
I love a good title.

01:20:44.470 --> 01:20:45.470
Tim Ferriss: Isn't it great?

01:20:45.470 --> 01:20:47.889
And it was like a family photo on the front.

01:20:47.889 --> 01:20:50.590
And I was like, what the fuck is going on
here?

01:20:50.590 --> 01:20:51.630
So I picked it up.

01:20:51.630 --> 01:21:01.260
And I will warn people in advance, trigger
warning for some people, there are some very

01:21:01.260 --> 01:21:05.409
graphic sex scenes in this poetry book.

01:21:05.409 --> 01:21:06.679
So there's that.

01:21:06.679 --> 01:21:07.679
But I was so impressed.

01:21:07.679 --> 01:21:12.250
I have not read his new collection yet, but
Night Sky with Exit Wounds, an apology to

01:21:12.250 --> 01:21:18.889
the author if I'm butchering your name, I
